  {
    "sha": "58672a5158e2b8f999e062f7d89538fcd29ea226",
    "node_id": "C_kwDONqx-A9oAKDU4NjcyYTUxNThlMmI4Zjk5OWUwNjJmN2Q4OTUzOGZjZDI5ZWEyMjY",
    "commit": {
      "author": {
        "name": "Andrew Waterman",
        "email": "andrew@sifive.com",
        "date": "2026-01-17T00:10:53Z"
      },
      "committer": {
        "name": "James Ball",
        "email": "jameball@qti.qualcomm.com",
        "date": "2026-01-23T00:31:17Z"
      },
      "message": "Clarify which vstart values are reserved for whole register instructions (#2572)\n\nThe usual property that the instructions are reserved when `vstart >= VLMAX`\ndoes not apply here; instead, the relevant effective maximum vector length\nfor these instructions is `evl`.  (Recall that these instructions always\noperate on whole registers, so effective vector length and effective VLMAX\nare equal.)\n\nNote that the spec had an additional statement no elements will be processed\nif `vstart >= evl`.  If we combine this with the property that\n`vstart >= evl` is reserved, the reserved property dominates, so we can\nremove the old clause.  The old behavior is still legal, as it is one valid\ninterpretation of \"reserved\".\n\nFixes #2564.",
      "tree": {
        "sha": "efde2f4f1f96528281b0240d34248ccdf64227c4",
        "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/riscv/riscv-isa-manual/git/trees/efde2f4f1f96528281b0240d34248ccdf64227c4"
